  One correction to what I described before implementing: I said "find the last space in the slice," which is subtly
  wrong. Searching [38;5;153mslice(0, budget)[39m means a cut landing [3mexactly[0m at a word end still backs off to the previous word —
  [38;5;153mformat("the quick brown fox", {maxLength: 12})[39m would give [38;5;153m"the..."[39m instead of [38;5;153m"the quick..."[39m. Searching [38;5;153mbudget + 1[39m
  treats a space sitting right at the boundary as the clean break. The test at [38;5;153mformat.test.js:15[39m is the one that caught
  it.

  [1m[38;5;153mformat.test.js[0m — 12 new assertions in the existing [38;5;153mconsole.assert[39m style: untouched-when-short, exact boundary,
  word-boundary cut, whole-words-to-budget, mid-word backoff, no-boundary hard fallback, whitespace trim, [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m of 3
  / 2 / 0, composition with [38;5;153mprefix[39m, composition with [38;5;153muppercase[39m.

  Verification: ran [38;5;153mnode format.test.js[39m before implementing — 10 of the 12 failed, each because the feature was missing
  (the two "untouched" cases correctly passed from the start). After implementing, all pass with no assertion output.

  Two notes:
